1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,297
Mix a few chemicals and let them react.

2
00:00:02,297 --> 00:00:04,595
Often they settle into one final state.

3
00:00:04,595 --> 00:00:09,838
But some reaction networks can settle into different states, depending on how they start.

4
00:00:09,838 --> 00:00:11,016
A bit like a switch.

5
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:17,499
So here's the question. With only a few chemicals and reactions, how many resting states

6
00:00:17,499 --> 00:00:23,289
can a network have? And how complicated must the reactions be to reach that maximum?

7
00:00:23,667 --> 00:00:29,846
Let's start from zero. Molecules drift around, and when a blue X meets a gold Y, they

8
00:00:29,846 --> 00:00:35,007
can react. How often? Three X's and two Y's make six possible meetings.

9
00:00:35,007 --> 00:00:38,787
Double the X's, and there are twelve: twice as fast.

10
00:00:39,167 --> 00:00:43,275
So write X and Y for how concentrated each chemical is.

11
00:00:43,275 --> 00:00:48,878
Then the rate is a fixed number, called the rate constant, times X times Y.

12
00:00:48,878 --> 00:00:51,119
That's the law of mass action.

13
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,844
Some reactions need three molecules at once, say two X's and a Y.

14
00:00:56,844 --> 00:01:00,462
Then the rate is a number times X squared Y.

15
00:01:00,462 --> 00:01:08,108
That's a three-body collision. With at most two molecules going in, call it a pair collision.

16
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:11,887
Now, what does settling mean? Picture a water tank.

17
00:01:11,887 --> 00:01:17,068
A tap fills it steadily, and a hole drains it, faster when the tank is fuller.

18
00:01:17,467 --> 00:01:21,499
The level rises until water leaves as fast as it arrives.

19
00:01:21,499 --> 00:01:25,319
Then it stops changing, even though water still flows.

20
00:01:25,319 --> 00:01:29,635
That's an equilibrium, or a resting state: the flows balance.

21
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:34,625
Plot both flows against the level. The tap is flat, and the drain rises.

22
00:01:34,625 --> 00:01:37,048
The resting level is where they cross.

23
00:01:37,048 --> 00:01:42,596
So counting resting states means counting crossings, and wiggly curves could cross more

24
00:01:42,596 --> 00:01:43,233
than once.

25
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,339
Now our running example, from the paper.

26
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:52,432
Two chemicals, two reactions. In the first, two X's and a Y collide, and a single X comes

27
00:01:52,432 --> 00:01:54,760
out. So one X and one Y disappear.

28
00:01:55,133 --> 00:02:00,683
In the second, two Y's make an X and three Y's, so one X and one Y appear.

29
00:02:00,683 --> 00:02:05,933
By mass action, the rates are one ninth of X squared Y, and Y squared.

30
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,103
Both reactions change X and Y by the same amount.

31
00:02:10,103 --> 00:02:15,770
Start with five X's and three Y's. The first reaction gives four and two.

32
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:20,194
The second gives five and three. X minus Y never changes.

33
00:02:20,194 --> 00:02:22,212
That's a conservation law.

34
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,689
Let's draw it. A mixture is a point: X across, Y up.

35
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:32,665
Since X minus Y is stuck at two, the mixture can only slide along this line.

36
00:02:32,665 --> 00:02:34,552
We'll call it the slice.

37
00:02:34,933 --> 00:02:41,957
Where can it rest? Where both reactions run equally fast: one ninth of X squared Y equals

38
00:02:41,957 --> 00:02:47,402
Y squared. Divide by Y, and Y equals X squared over nine: a parabola.

39
00:02:47,402 --> 00:02:49,453
Call it the balance curve.

40
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:56,562
A resting state lies on both. So X squared over nine equals X minus two, which tidies

41
00:02:56,562 --> 00:03:00,837
up and factorises as X minus three, times X minus six.

42
00:03:00,837 --> 00:03:04,953
Two resting states: three and one, and six and four.

43
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:08,308
Both are clean crossings, not mere touches.

44
00:03:08,308 --> 00:03:12,944
That's called nondegenerate. Nudge the slice, and a touch vanishes.

45
00:03:12,944 --> 00:03:16,541
A clean crossing just slides a little, and survives.

46
00:03:16,933 --> 00:03:24,137
Now the big picture. Murad Banaji and Elisenda Feliu proved that any network with n chemicals,

47
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:30,498
n reactions and one conservation law has at most n clean resting states on a slice.

48
00:03:30,498 --> 00:03:32,797
Ours reaches the maximum: two.

49
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:36,724
Then they asked: is that maximum reached for every n?

50
00:03:36,724 --> 00:03:41,087
And how many molecules must collide in one reaction to get there?

51
00:03:41,467 --> 00:03:47,275
The new result answers both. For every n from two up, a network reaches exactly n, with

52
00:03:47,275 --> 00:03:49,811
collisions of at most three molecules.

53
00:03:49,811 --> 00:03:54,151
And three is the least possible. Two chemicals was already known.

54
00:03:54,151 --> 00:03:55,219
The rest is new.

55
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:00,530
Here's a puzzle. Why does our example need a three-body collision?

56
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:04,415
Think about the reaction that destroys an X and a Y.

57
00:04:04,415 --> 00:04:06,208
Pause, and have a think.

58
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:10,561
A reaction can only destroy molecules that went into it.

59
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:16,503
So that collision needs an X and a Y. With at most two molecules, it must be exactly

60
00:04:16,503 --> 00:04:17,352
one of each.

61
00:04:17,733 --> 00:04:23,865
Balance it against any other pair collision, and the balance curve is a line from the

62
00:04:23,865 --> 00:04:27,543
corner, a falling curve, or a flat or upright line.

63
00:04:27,543 --> 00:04:30,789
Each one meets our rising slice at most once.

64
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:35,284
With three molecules, the collision can be two X's and a Y.

65
00:04:35,284 --> 00:04:41,356
That X squared bends the balance curve into a parabola, and a parabola can cross a line

66
00:04:41,356 --> 00:04:41,775
twice.

67
00:04:42,167 --> 00:04:48,157
If the slice sloped down instead, the balance curve never would, so again, one crossing.

68
00:04:48,157 --> 00:04:53,876
For bigger networks, Banaji and Feliu proved pair collisions give at most two, which

69
00:04:53,876 --> 00:04:56,327
falls short from three chemicals up.

70
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:02,125
Now three chemicals: X, Y and Z. Two X's and a Y make an X.

71
00:05:02,125 --> 00:05:09,204
Two Y's and a Z make a Y and two Z's. And two Z's make an X, two Y's and a Z.

72
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:16,137
When can this rest? X is used by the first reaction and made by the third, so those two

73
00:05:16,137 --> 00:05:21,998
must match. Z is made by the second and used by the third, so those match too.

74
00:05:21,998 --> 00:05:23,952
All three rates are equal.

75
00:05:24,333 --> 00:05:28,005
Two conditions on three numbers leave one free dial.

76
00:05:28,005 --> 00:05:34,713
Call it t. The resting states are exactly the points where X is sixty t cubed, Y is eighty-five

77
00:05:34,713 --> 00:05:37,749
t squared, and Z is eleven t to the fourth.

78
00:05:38,133 --> 00:05:44,091
Check the first rate. Sixty squared times eighty-five is three hundred and six thousand,

79
00:05:44,091 --> 00:05:48,424
exactly the number underneath, so only t to the eighth survives.

80
00:05:48,424 --> 00:05:51,741
The other two rates give t to the eighth as well.

81
00:05:52,133 --> 00:05:56,885
Every reaction here leaves Y plus Z minus X unchanged.

82
00:05:56,885 --> 00:06:00,317
That's this network's conservation law.

83
00:06:00,317 --> 00:06:04,541
Let's take the slice where it equals thirty-six.

84
00:06:04,933 --> 00:06:11,560
Walk along the resting curve and track that quantity: eleven t to the fourth, minus sixty

85
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:17,964
t cubed, plus eighty-five t squared. A sum of powers like this is called a polynomial.

86
00:06:17,964 --> 00:06:20,197
When does it equal thirty-six?

87
00:06:20,567 --> 00:06:24,768
It crosses the line at t equals one, two and three.

88
00:06:24,768 --> 00:06:27,980
Three resting states, the most allowed.

89
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:31,523
The first is sixty, eighty-five and eleven.

90
00:06:31,523 --> 00:06:36,383
Check: eighty-five plus eleven, minus sixty, is thirty-six.

91
00:06:36,767 --> 00:06:40,607
But could a fourth crossing be hiding further along?

92
00:06:40,607 --> 00:06:46,441
Move everything to one side, and read only the signs: plus, minus, plus, minus.

93
00:06:46,441 --> 00:06:47,327
Three flips.

94
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:53,545
In sixteen thirty-seven, René Descartes showed that a polynomial has no more positive

95
00:06:53,545 --> 00:06:57,259
solutions than sign flips. Three flips, at most three.

96
00:06:57,259 --> 00:07:02,004
We have three, and a touch would count twice, so all three are clean.

97
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:08,694
Clean crossings are robust, too. Shift the slice a little, or the rate constants, and

98
00:07:08,694 --> 00:07:13,656
the crossings slide but survive. It's not a knife-edge coincidence.

99
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:19,466
Here's the recipe for n chemicals. Each reaction's input is two of one chemical, plus

100
00:07:19,466 --> 00:07:22,981
one of the next. The last is just two of the final one.

101
00:07:22,981 --> 00:07:25,601
Never more than three molecules going in.

102
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,097
As before, the resting states lie on one curve, with every concentration a different

103
00:07:32,097 --> 00:07:38,266
power of t. So the conservation law gives a polynomial with n plus one terms: at most

104
00:07:38,266 --> 00:07:40,952
n sign flips, so at most n crossings.

105
00:07:41,333 --> 00:07:46,387
Now run it backwards. Choose a polynomial that's zero at one, two, up to n.

106
00:07:46,387 --> 00:07:52,047
Its numbers then fix each reaction's output and rate, and the paper proves these are

107
00:07:52,047 --> 00:07:53,597
always valid reactions.

108
00:07:53,967 --> 00:07:57,084
For four chemicals, here's the polynomial.

109
00:07:57,084 --> 00:08:01,685
Four sign flips, and zero at exactly one, two, three and four.

110
00:08:01,685 --> 00:08:03,615
Four clean resting states.

111
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,345
Now, what this doesn't say. Resting is not the same as stable.

112
00:08:08,345 --> 00:08:14,372
In our two-chemical example, the flow along the slice runs away from the first resting

113
00:08:14,372 --> 00:08:18,016
state, and into the second. A hilltop, and a valley.

114
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:23,715
The theorem counts resting states. It says nothing about how many can be stable at once,

115
00:08:23,715 --> 00:08:25,768
which is what a real switch needs.

116
00:08:26,167 --> 00:08:29,157
Also, the conserved quantity has a minus sign.

117
00:08:29,157 --> 00:08:33,578
Unlike the total mass in a sealed flask, its slice runs off forever.

118
00:08:33,578 --> 00:08:39,168
Banaji and Feliu showed a slice that doesn't run off allows at most two, so beyond two

119
00:08:39,168 --> 00:08:41,119
chemicals, that's unavoidable.

120
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:46,779
And three molecules is a limit on inputs only: one output here has four.

121
00:08:46,779 --> 00:08:53,012
This is mathematics about the mass action equations, not a mixture anyone has made in

122
00:08:53,012 --> 00:08:53,452
a lab.

123
00:08:53,833 --> 00:09:00,021
So how sure can we be? The result for every n rests on a written proof, checked by internal

124
00:09:00,021 --> 00:09:05,869
reviewers. Two independent programs also rebuilt the networks for n from two to eight,

125
00:09:05,869 --> 00:09:09,337
and checked all thirty-five resting states exactly.

126
00:09:09,733 --> 00:09:14,787
Those checks catch errors in the formulas, but a few cases can't prove every n.

127
00:09:14,787 --> 00:09:20,416
That's the proof's job. Outside experts haven't reviewed it yet, and a literature search

128
00:09:20,416 --> 00:09:24,637
found no earlier recipe for every n, though it can't rule one out.

129
00:09:25,033 --> 00:09:29,605
To recap. Resting states are crossings of a balance curve and a slice.

130
00:09:29,605 --> 00:09:35,286
With n chemicals and n reactions, three-body collisions reach the maximum, n, for every

131
00:09:35,286 --> 00:09:37,441
n. And pair collisions never can.

132
00:09:37,833 --> 00:09:43,489
Still open: how many of these resting states can be stable at the same time, which a

133
00:09:43,489 --> 00:09:49,279
real switch needs. And whether the outputs, which grow with n in this recipe, could be

134
00:09:49,279 --> 00:09:50,289
kept small too.

135
00:09:50,667 --> 00:09:52,371
Thanks for watching.
